.TH HUBLOT 1 "2026" "hublot" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hublot \- terminal UI for a local Docker daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hublot
.RI [ flags ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hublot
is a full-screen terminal interface for the Docker daemon running on the local
machine: live CPU, memory, network and block IO per container, keyboard-driven
navigation into containers, images, volumes, networks and Compose projects, and
every action the Engine API exposes without leaving the interface.
.PP
Two things set it apart from similar tools. It treats disk hygiene as a first
class concern: a view of where disk space went, pruning per object category, and
a preview of exactly what a prune would destroy, reconstructed object by object
because the Engine API offers no dry run. And it is Compose aware: stacks are
rebuilt from the labels Compose writes, grouped by project and service, with
drift detection that tells you whether the running containers still match the
YAML on disk.
.PP
.B hublot
talks to the local daemon socket only. Remote hosts, contexts, TCP and SSH
transports are deliberately out of scope.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.B \-read\-only
Disable every action that changes anything. Destructive keys are greyed out and
a badge appears in the status bar. Use this to look at a server safely.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
Lowercase is safe, uppercase is destructive or forced.
.TP
.B 1..6, tab, shift+tab, left, right
Switch view. The tabs run containers, compose, images, volumes, networks,
disk: stacks are how hosts are run, so they come before the loose objects
underneath them.
.TP
.B /
Incremental filter. Every space-separated term must match.
.TP
.B s, A
Cycle the sort column, reverse the direction.
.TP
.B space, a
Select the row under the cursor, or everything the filter left visible. A
selection is what makes an action apply to several rows at once: select, then
press
.BR S ,
.B R
or
.B D
and it runs on all of them. Selected rows carry a bar in the left margin, the
header counts them, and
.B esc
clears the selection.
.TP
.B enter, l, e
Detail pane, logs, shell inside the container.
.TP
.B u, S, R, P, K, D
Start, stop, restart, pause or unpause, kill with a signal, remove.
.TP
.B n
Create something: a container from a form in the container and image views, a
stack from a compose file in the compose view.
.TP
.B P
In the image view, pull any reference by name.
.TP
.B x
Contextual action palette, searchable by typing. It carries everything without
a key of its own: on a container, rename, cpu and memory limits, processes,
filesystem changes, commit, export, copying files in and out, and attaching or
detaching networks; on an image, tag, untag, save and load; on a volume or a
network, creating one.
.TP
.B t, ?, r, q
Task panel, help, force refresh, quit.
.SH COMPOSE
Each project is a line of its own above its services, and that line is the
whole stack. Actions apply to the row under the cursor:
.BR l ,
.BR u ,
.B S
and
.B R
act on everything from the project line and on one service from a service line.
.B enter
folds a stack, from its project line only.
.B v
reads the compose file in the panel without opening an editor, and
.B c
shows the configuration compose resolves from it.
.B L
reads the whole stack's logs from anywhere, and
.B n
brings up a stack from a compose file nothing has been created from yet.
.SH EDITING
.B E
opens a project's compose file in your editor, and offers to apply what you
wrote when it closes.
.B n
does the same for a path that holds no compose file yet: the directory and a
starter file are created, opened, and offered up.
.PP
Paths typed into hublot understand
.B ~
and
.BR $VARIABLES ,
and a relative one is taken from the directory hublot was started in.
.PP
What happened is read from the file, not from the editor: quitting without
writing, with
.B :q
or otherwise, changes nothing and is told so, and a stack created that way is
taken back out, starter file and directory included. Only a file whose contents
actually differ brings up the question of applying it.
.PP
The editor is asked for once and remembered in the configuration file. The
answer comes from
.I editor
there, then from
.I $VISUAL
and
.IR $EDITOR ,
and otherwise from a list of what is installed: terminal editors first, since
they open in this window, with an entry for typing any other command. An editor
that opens its own window needs whatever makes it wait, and hublot adds that
flag for the ones it knows.
.SH THE SIDE PANEL
Logs, inspect output, process lists and resolved configuration open in a panel
beside the list rather than instead of it, so the thing being read stays next to
the thing it is about.
.TP
.B ctrl+w
Move the keyboard between the list and the panel.
.TP
.B W
Cycle how much of the screen the panel takes: a third, a half, two thirds, all
of it. The divider itself can be dragged with the mouse to any size in between.
.TP
.B esc
Close the panel.
.PP
With the panel focused,
.B /
searches it,
.B n
and
.B N
walk the matches,
.B f
follows a live stream and
.B w
wraps long lines.
.PP
On a narrow terminal the panel moves below the list, and on a small one it takes
the screen: the split is chosen from the size, not from a setting. The mouse
works throughout: the wheel scrolls, a click selects a row or focuses the panel,
and a click on the tab bar switches view.
.PP
The help overlay lists every binding and is generated from the same definitions
the program dispatches on, so it cannot fall out of date.
.SH THE SHELL
.B e
opens a shell inside the container under the cursor, full screen and on a
screen of its own: it does not open on top of whatever was in the terminal
before, and leaves nothing behind when it closes.
.B exit
or ctrl-d comes back to
.BR hublot ,
which refreshes, since a shell can have changed anything.
.PP
The shell run inside the container is bash when the image has one and sh
otherwise. Set
.B shell
in the configuration file to run something else.
.SH PAUSED CONTAINERS
A paused container is frozen where it stands and still in memory. It never
stopped, so it cannot be started:
.B P
is what lets it run again. The key bar reads
.B unpause
whenever the cursor is on a paused container, and starting one anyway says the
same thing rather than only passing on the daemon's refusal.
.SH WHEN SOMETHING FAILS
A failed action shows the daemon's own message, which is accurate but written
for someone who already knows what went wrong.
.B hublot
recognises the usual mistakes and puts a plain explanation above it: a host port
already bound, a container name already used, an image that cannot be pulled, a
compose variable with no value, a port below 1024, a full disk.
.PP
For a port clash it also names the container currently publishing that port, so
the answer is on screen rather than two commands away. The daemon's message is
never replaced, and nothing is claimed about a failure
.B hublot
does not recognise.
.PP
When the failure is in a file, the dialog offers to open it:
.B e
hands the compose file to your editor, and what you wrote is applied after a
confirmation, running again the command that failed. A compose file that will
not parse is named by compose itself, so this is the file the error is about
and not a guess.
.PP
The output of a long command stays in the task panel, which
.B t
opens.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/hublot/config.yaml
Optional configuration. Every setting has a working default, so the file may be
absent. Recognised keys: theme, stop_timeout, max_stat_streams, log_tail, shell,
read_only, default_sort, sort_descending, confirm_destructive, editor.
.IP
.B hublot
writes this file itself when it learns something worth keeping, such as the
editor you picked the first time you edited a compose file.
.TP
.I /var/run/docker.sock
The daemon socket of a root installation. If DOCKER_HOST names a unix socket,
that one is used instead; otherwise, when the path above does not exist,
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/docker.sock
is tried, which is where a rootless daemon listens.
.SH PERMISSIONS
.B hublot
enforces nothing of its own: the socket's mode is the access control, exactly as
it is for
.BR docker (1).
A user in the group that owns the socket, usually
.IR docker ,
uses hublot without sudo; anyone else is refused before a single request is
sent, and is told which of the three situations they are in: not in the group,
in it but from a session that started before the membership was granted, or in
it and refused for another reason.
.PP
Being in that group is equivalent to root on the host, since it allows mounting
any path into a container. Running
.B sudo hublot
works as well, with the usual consequences: the configuration read is root's,
under
.IR /root/.config ,
and files written by saving an image or copying out of a container belong to
root.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hublot
exits with 0 when the interface closes normally, and 1 when the daemon cannot be
reached or the configuration file cannot be parsed.
